Show that, if <span class="math inline">\(n\)</span> is an integer such that
<span class="math display">\[\begin{equation}
(n-3)^3+n^3=(n+3)^3,\label{eq:1}
\end{equation}\]</span>
<p>then <span class="math inline">\(n\)</span> is even and <span class="math inline">\(n^2\)</span> is a factor of <span class="math inline">\(54\)</span>. Deduce that there is no integer <span class="math inline">\(n\)</span> which satisfies the equation <span class="math inline">\(\eqref{eq:1}\)</span>.</p>
Show that, if <span class="math inline">\(n\)</span> is an integer such that
<span class="math display">\[\begin{equation}
(n-6)^3+n^3=(n+6)^3,\label{eq:2}
\end{equation}\]</span>
<p>then <span class="math inline">\(n\)</span> is even. Deduce that there is no integer <span class="math inline">\(n\)</span> which satisfies the equation <span class="math inline">\(\eqref{eq:2}\)</span>.</p>
